This source code is how the PhoneCanDo app lets AI agents see and use a
phone. It is published so you can read it, check it, contribute to it and
run it for yourself as this license allows. Every right not expressly
granted below is reserved.

1. Definitions

"Copyright holder" means Krishna Kushwaha, anyone who later holds the
copyright in the Software by a written assignment or by inheritance, and
anyone either of them authorises, in a document they sign, to act for them
under this license.

"Official repository" means https://github.com/phonecando/phone-control.

"Official website" means https://phonecando.app.

"Official release" means the source code of a release the copyright holder
tags in the official repository, or of the official repository's default
branch, as the copyright holder publishes it there. Pull requests, other
branches, forks, and commits that only forks contain are not official
releases, even where GitHub shows them under the official repository's
address.

"Software" means the material the copyright holder publishes in the
official repository, apart from what section 2 leaves out, and anything
built from it: source code, builds and other binaries, text and
documentation, in whole or in part. Third-party components (section 10)
are not part of the Software.

"Release repository" means https://github.com/phonecando/distribution,
where the copyright holder publishes the official app's releases.

"Official app" means the PhoneCanDo app as the copyright holder publishes it
through its official channels (the official website, the release
repository's releases and app stores it publishes to).

"Modified version" means any changed, extended, translated, ported or
derived version of the Software, or any work that includes part of it,
except your own files and work under section 11.

"Personal use" means use by you alone, as an individual, on devices you own
or control, and not for any employer, client, business or organisation.

"Contribution" means a pull request, patch or issue submitted to the
official repository.

"Written permission" means a document signed by the copyright holder, on
paper or with an electronic signature, that names you and says what it
allows.

2. What this license does not cover

The official app is not licensed under this license. You install and use it
under the PhoneCanDo End User License Agreement, which it shows you and
which you accept in it. That covers the official app's files published in
the release repository (its APKs, update manifests and other builds) and
the copy of the Software inside the official app. The release repository's
own files are not part of the Software either; its LICENSE says what
covers them.

The source archives of third-party components published in the release
repository keep their own licenses (section 10).

The rest of the official app's source code is not published, and this
license gives no right to it.

3. The source code

You may:
- View, clone and study the source code, and check how it works and how
  safe it is.
- Fork the official repository on GitHub, as GitHub's Terms of Service
  allow. Beyond those Terms, this license lets a fork hold only changes you
  have submitted as contributions, or have been preparing for less than 60
  days.
- Build the Software and run it on up to three devices of your own to
  develop and test a contribution, while you prepare it and while it waits
  for a decision under section 4b. Such builds are for testing that change,
  not for everyday use or an organisation's ongoing work; everyday use falls
  under section 4.
- Submit contributions to the official repository.

These uses are allowed even if someone pays you for them.

Nothing in this license limits the rights GitHub's Terms of Service give
GitHub, its affiliates and its users over the official repository, and this
license gives no right beyond them.

4. Your own builds

a. Unmodified: you may build an official release, unmodified, and use that
   build for your personal use. Choosing among the build options an official
   release itself offers is not a modification.
b. Modified: you may use a modified version for your personal use only while
   every change in it that is not in an official release was submitted by
   you, complete and in good faith, as a contribution (section 7), and is
   still waiting for a decision. Once the copyright holder merges one of
   those contributions, with or without changes, or declines it, you must
   stop using every build that contains your version of it within 30 days.
   You may then use a build of an official release that includes what was
   merged. A contribution counts as declined when it is closed without being
   merged, for any reason, or when 180 days pass after it was submitted
   without a merge. Submitting a contribution again, or one much like a
   declined one, does not start a new period.
c. No build, modified or not, may bypass, unlock or remove paid features,
   limits tied to a paid plan, license checks or this license's notices.

5. What you may not do

Unless you have written permission from the copyright holder, you may not:

a. Use the Software, or any part of the source code, for any commercial
   purpose. This includes selling it, offering it as a paid or free app or
   service, using it to earn money from ads, subscriptions or donations,
   using it inside a company or organisation, and using it for client work.
   This does not limit the uses section 3 allows.
b. Use the Software in any way sections 3 and 4 do not allow. Viewing the
   official website and the official repository, and sharing links to them,
   are always allowed.
c. Share the Software, or a modified version, with anyone: not friends,
   family, classmates, coworkers or your team, not in private and not in
   public, not for free and not for money. This covers sending or copying
   files, builds or code by any means (files, repositories, app stores,
   chats or devices), including builds section 4 allows. It does not stop
   you from:
   - keeping a GitHub fork as section 3 allows;
   - sharing links to the official website, the official repository or
     the release repository;
   - quoting short excerpts of the source code, with their file paths, or
     sharing screenshots, to talk about PhoneCanDo or to report or discuss
     how safe it is;
   - sharing your own files and work as section 11 allows.
d. Make a modified version for any purpose sections 3 and 4 do not allow,
   or publish one, except in a GitHub fork as section 3 allows.
e. Copy any part of the Software into another project, product or service.
f. Use the source code or documentation of the Software (the files in the
   official repository, not the official website's pages) to train or
   fine-tune machine learning models, as data to test or benchmark them, or
   in a dataset. This is a reservation of rights under
   Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790 and every similar law. Using an
   AI tool to help you read the code, check how safe it is or prepare a
   contribution is allowed.
g. Sublicense the Software or grant anyone else any right to it.
h. Remove or change this license or any copyright notice in a copy or build
   you keep or share. Proposing a change to them in a contribution is
   allowed.

